===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}} {{term-label|en|computing|humorous|informal}} 

# A [[method]] of [[transfer#Verb|transferring]] a [[computer]] [[file#Noun|file]] from one computer to another by [[copy#Verb|copying]] it to a [[floppy disk]], [[thumb drive]], or some other [[external]] [[storage]] [[device]], [[carry#Verb|carrying]] the device to the other computer, and [[save#Verb|saving]] the file there, in contrast to [[electronic]] methods used by [[networked#Adjective|networked]] computers to transfer [[data]]. {{defdate|from 1980s}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|magazine=Hardcopy|location=Brea, Calif.|publisher=Sedlin Pub. Co.|year=1986|volume=6|page=143|column=3|issn=0279-8123|oclc=479346585|passage=Significant advances are occurring to race past '''sneakernet'''. But the ideal computer interconnect solution will always be just around the next bend.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=David Plotkin|title=Compute! Specific|magazine={{w|Compute!}}|location=|publisher={{w|Small System Services}}|location=Greensboro, N.C.|publisher=COMPUTE! Publications, [[w:American Broadcasting Company|ABC Publishing]]|month=April|year=1989|volume=11|issue=4, issue 107|page=58|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/1989-04-compute-magazine/page/n59/mode/1up|column=2|issn=0194-357X|oclc=473378905|passage=Before complicated networks and E-mail and special-interest bulletin boards, there was '''Sneakernet'''. '''Sneakernet''' required no special protocol, no special cables and terminal software. To communicate on '''Sneakernet''', all you needed to do was take a disk from your computer to someone else's, stick the disk in that remote drive, and copy some files—instant data transfer. Well, almost instant.}}
# The [[group#Noun|group]] of computers involved in this [[practice]].

